      "id": 1,
      "claim": "Both ships can generate the same maximum decelerating force when stopping.",
      "evidence": "The problem states the ships are identical except for cargo; braking is done by the ships' own engines/reverse thrust, which produce a force independent of the load carried.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The ship with heavy cargo has a larger total mass than the ship with light cargo.",
      "evidence": "Heavy cargo adds mass; the hulls are identical, so the only mass difference is the cargo.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Because both ships travel at the same speed, the heavier ship has greater linear momentum (p = m v).",
      "evidence": "Momentum formula p = m v; v is equal for both, m_heavy > m_light.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The time required to stop is given by Δt = Δp / F (impulse‑momentum theorem).",
      "evidence": "From Newton's second law, impulse F Δt equals change in momentum Δp; solving for Δt gives Δt = Δp / F.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Since the heavier ship has larger initial momentum and the decelerating force is the same, its stopping time is longer.",
      "evidence": "Insert larger Δp into Δt = Δp / F while keeping F constant; Δt increases proportionally.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "B",
  "confidence": 0.98,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "A",
      "reason_rejected": "Increased friction would increase the decelerating force, which would tend to shorten, not lengthen, the stopping time."
    },
    {
      "answer": "C",
      "reason_rejected": "Greater acceleration (deceleration) means a larger magnitude of negative acceleration, which would reduce stopping time, opposite to the observed effect."
    },
    {
      "answer": "D",
      "reason_rejected": "Constant velocity is a description of the initial motion, not a cause of a difference in stopping time."
    }
  ]
